# Trilio Dynamic Mount Service runtime directory - TVAULT-7655.
# Managed by the trilio-datamover Ansible role. Do not edit by hand.
#
# Why this file exists
# --------------------
# /run is a tmpfs, so /run/dms is gone after every node reboot and has to be
# recreated before the DMS container starts. Creating it once from an Ansible
# "file:" task cannot work: edpm_container_manage generates
# edpm_triliovault-dms.service as WantedBy=multi-user.target with
# Restart=always, so the container comes back on every boot into a /run that
# was just emptied - and because it runs as 42436 under a root-owned /run it
# can never recreate the directory itself. The service then stays dead until
# someone re-runs the data plane deployment.
#
# systemd-tmpfiles-setup.service is ordered Before=sysinit.target, and
# multi-user.target pulls in basic.target -> sysinit.target first, so this
# always runs before any container unit.
#
# Why it must be on the host and not baked into the DMS image
# -----------------------------------------------------------
# The DMS server must be able to read the pid and lock files written by a
# previous instance of the container. The FUSE mounts it manages outlive the
# container (vault_data_dir is bind-mounted :shared onto the host), so its
# record of which s3vaultfuse process owns which backup target has to outlive
# it as well. An image layer lands in the container's writable overlay, which
# podman discards on every recreate. Losing the contents on a *reboot* is
# fine; losing them on a *recreate* is not.
#
# Notes
# -----
# uid/gid are numeric on purpose: the host is not required to have a nova user
# at 42436, only the container is.
#
# The age field must stay "-". Any value there makes
# systemd-tmpfiles-clean.timer delete files under /run/dms on a schedule,
# which would remove live pid and lock files out from under a running mount.
d /run/dms       0755 42436 42436 -
d /run/dms/s3    0755 42436 42436 -
d /run/dms/locks 0755 42436 42436 -
